**Entries and page fragments.** HTML pages come from directories that contain
`@entry.moth`. A direct top-level template in that file contributes page output.
Direct const root `$in` inserts supply builder metadata. Exact entry and metadata rules live in Project Structure. This
page describes the template and value boundary; current support is tracked in
@../progress (Progress) and @../progress/packages-and-builders (Packages and builders).

Templates create owned `String` values.

They use square brackets. Collections and maps use braces.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    name = "Priya"

    greeting = [:
        Hello, [name]
    ]
]

### Head and body

A colon separates the template head from its body.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    ["prefix": body]
]

The head may contain:

- a value or helper template
- directives
- slot declarations and insert contributions
- final `if` or `loop` control-flow suffixes

The final `if` suffix conditionally includes one body for a true `Bool` or a
present option capture. The final `loop` suffix repeats its body with an
ordinary loop header. Template control flow excludes `else`, `else if`,
`break` and `continue`. Use ordinary Moth code for richer decisions.

The body contains authored text and nested templates.

Authored `.moth` templates must close with a closing square bracket.

Truncated heads, bodies, nested children and directive-argument templates are
syntax errors.

### Capture and text

A template may read visible values from its surrounding scope.

Inside a template body:

- backticks are ordinary body text
- backslashes are ordinary body text
- formatter directives decide how authored text is transformed

Double-quoted string slices still use normal escaping in expression position.

### Literal delimiters in output

To render a literal open or close square bracket inside a template body, insert
a string slice:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    content = [: ["[literal]"]]
]

The current compiler does not support backtick raw string slices, so string
insertion is the supported way to emit literal square brackets.

### Compile-time and runtime forms

A template may:

- fully fold to a compile-time string
- remain as runtime string construction

The source-level result stays an owned `String`.

Templates do not become source-visible function values.

Runtime fixed binary-float interpolation and compile-time folding use the same
Moth formatter.

### MON data boundary

Unevaluated template syntax is not a MON string literal. A folded compile-time template and an evaluated runtime template are each an ordinary serialisable `String` for their producer; which side produced the text never changes document validity. There is no automatic MON interpolation and no runtime template producer is exported by the Rust codec. That contract is Rust-only serialised-data documentation. See @../mon/#mon-format (MON format).

### Page fragments

A direct top-level template contributes output only in `@entry.moth`.

- top-level runtime templates run through the entry runtime in source order
- direct top-level const templates fold into compile-time fragments
- a direct top-level const `$in` insert routes to a builder-registered destination
- ordinary direct const fragments and runtime body fragments render into the page body
- a template assigned to a binding or returned from a function remains a `String`
  value and does not become a page fragment by itself

Only a direct root insert reaches builder routing. A nested or stored insert stays
ordinary template composition. Runtime top-level named builder inserts are not
part of the contract.

Page assembly is owned by Project Structure. Const-template rules, including
builder slot contributions, are owned by @../constants/#const-templates (Const
templates). See @../project-structure/#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder
slots).
